Add i18n, the settings page, and a real schema migrator

Three of the remaining items from the delivery plan. Each one had a specific failure
mode that a naive implementation would have introduced; those are what the tests pin.

## i18n: translate what a human outside the project reads, and nothing else

Scope is deliberately narrow. Translated: bot replies in a chat (refusals, the [voice]
label and transcript prefix), API `detail` strings negotiated from Accept-Language, and
the console UI (per browser, persisted). Logs and stack traces stay English.

That boundary is enforced, not documented: tests/test_i18n.py walks the AST looking for
`logger.<level>(t(...))`. A translated log line means the same event has different text
per locale, which silently breaks grep and every alert rule built on it. Rules written
in prose do not survive a busy week; this one is a failing build.

Language travels on the message, not the process. PipelineContext.locale is set by the
runtime and overridable per persona card, because one process serves several groups and
a module-level "current locale" would make a reply depend on whichever message was
handled last.

INJECTION_REFUSAL stays as a constant for callers with no locale, and gained
injection_refusal(locale) for the two paths that refuse a message. One function, so
typed text and transcribed speech cannot drift into different answers to the same
attempt.

## Page 12: settings, with the writability boundary as the point

Four independent cards, each saving on its own - no page-level save button, because the
settings are unrelated and one button would imply a transaction that does not exist.

The write path is a closed allow-list (locale, two voice switches, voice name), not
"any environment variable". host, data_dir, master_key and group_allowlist are refused
by name: those reshape the security boundary, and a form field that lets a panel token
holder redraw it is privilege escalation with a nicer UI.

.env is rewritten line by line, not regenerated. It is the operator's file: it holds
comments explaining why a value is what it is, and variables this build does not read.
Regenerating would delete both silently, and they would find out next time they opened
it.

SOAPBOT_ENV_FILE now relocates the file Settings reads. Without it, a service started
by systemd or a container has a working directory that is not the repo, so the panel
would save to a .env nobody reads and report success.

## Migrations: create_all cannot change a column

"Startup is deterministic, no CLI step" is true for a fresh database and false at the
first schema change: create_all skips an existing table, so an upgraded binary queries
columns the file does not have - in production, on real conversation history, with no
recorded version to reason from.

memory/migrations.py carries each change forward one step at a time and records it in
the same transaction. A database newer than the build refuses to start, naming both
versions, rather than writing old-schema assumptions into newer rows. MIGRATIONS is
empty today (v1 is the initial schema); tests/test_migrations.py registers a step and
asserts it runs exactly once so the loop is exercised rather than assumed.

## Two defects found by verifying in a browser rather than in tests

The settings page passed typecheck, build and API tests while being wrong in a way only
a person would notice: saving a toggle made it snap back to the old value. The write had
succeeded (the .env was correct) but the page reloaded the running process's values,
which are the pre-save ones until restart - so it looked like the save had failed and
the operator would click again. Controls now keep the chosen value and show a
"restart to apply" badge until a restart, which is also the honest version of "saved but
not live".

The same pass caught the first load rendering empty selects: useAsync fires on mount, so
the drafts were seeded from nothing. Now seeded once from the first payload, and
deliberately not re-seeded on later refreshes, which would clobber edits in progress.

Verified end to end in Edge against a real server: switched the console language (nav,
headings, document.title and <html lang> all followed), toggled voice transcription and
saw the .env gain one line with the operator's own comment and port line intact, then
confirmed the process still reported false, then restarted and confirmed it reported
true.

959 passed, 8 skipped; ruff clean; vue-tsc clean; vite build clean; check_contrast and
check_deploy unchanged; mypy 53 errors, identical to baseline.
